WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.100
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Dr. Eli Merritt, who's a political historian

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.250
at Vanderbilt University and also editor of
the recently released collection of essays

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:15.769
The Curse of Demagogues Lessons Learned from
the Presidency of Donald J. Trump.

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:17.250
It's really great to have you on.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.250
I appreciate your time.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.250
Speaker 2: Thanks.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.250
Good to be here, David.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.470
Speaker 1: So I think one place to start would
be one of the big discussions that's now happening

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.880
in the aftermath of the Trump presidency.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.509
And we're still waiting to know whether there
will be criminal charges or not.

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:34.200
And that may be part of this conversation.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:40.020
Did we learn that the checks and balances
and safeguards put in place by the founders

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.070
were not strong enough?

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.390
If we had a president interested and willing
to do the things Trump did?

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.460
Or is that too bold a statement to make right
now?

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.710
Speaker 2: Well, I think we learned that lesson
in something else.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:59.539
The the checks and balances that were put
in place by the founders back over 200 years

00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:05.299
ago, as you know, with the Electoral College
and the powers of impeachment and conviction

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:07.290
and disbarment from future office.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.290
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.479
Speaker 2: For very long, the Electoral College
has not worked.

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:18.780
And so the real truth is, soon after the Constitutional
Convention, 1787 ratification, 1788 political

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.560
parties became the dominant instruments of
our politics.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:27.310
And also they became the dominant instrument
of checks and balances against demagogues

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.890
and authoritarians making it to the presidency.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:37.650
So many things, I believe, failed in the years
2015 until continuing today.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.250
But the most significant failure of checks
and balances, the most significant failure

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:47.920
of gatekeepers against demagogues who become
authoritarians is clearly the political parties.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.060
Our political parties are woefully weak today.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.010
And we can talk more about sort of the origins
of that.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:59.340
But they became relatively emasculated starting
in the early 1970s when all checks and balances

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.710
at the nominating conventions were removed.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.320
Speaker 1: Let's talk a little bit more about
that.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.729
You know, I, I have not found anyone who disagrees
with me that Donald Trump could not have won

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:12.170
a Democratic primary.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:17.510
Now, the reasons why people might disagree
with my reason is a little bit crude, which

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:22.450
is even though I'm neither a Republican nor
a Democrat, I don't believe Democrats would

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:28.620
have fallen for what Trump was offering, whereas
the Republican electorate, by definition,

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.480
did fall for it in that they made him the
nominee in 2016.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:39.400
Is that a fair is that one of the differences
right now between the parties in terms of

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.760
the constituents?

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.900
And then are the differences between the constituents
greater than the differences between the parties

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:46.930
structures?

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:53.300
Speaker 2: I would say it seems very clear
right now at this point in our history that

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.460
the Democratic Party is the party of the Constitution,
the rule of law.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.800
It is the party of ethical leadership.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.221
No party is perfect and no and no.

00:03:03.221 --> 00:03:11.680
And the entirety of the political figures,
politicians and any party are not ethical.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.570
But I think it's a hard question to ask.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:21.940
Democrats would never elect Trump, but I think
the most important thing to clarify here is

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:27.950
that we have descended somewhat into what
I like to refer to as a demagogue culture.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:35.360
And I would not ever remove the possibility
that as the culture and the politics become

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:42.530
more and more fierce, that some liberal leaning
demagogue might come along who has similar

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:47.500
tendencies and angers and frustrations and
a personality as fierce as Trump's.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.950
And if we get into this black and white thinking,
we think the other side is evil.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:56.320
I do believe Democrats are also susceptible
to demagogues.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.849
That's why what we do in one party is something
we should do in other parties.

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:05.450
And again, I think the most important area
of intervention, the most flexible point of

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:09.090
intervention, is the presidential nominating
system.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:13.129
A lot of people don't know that very little
law governs, certainly not.

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:16.479
The Constitution governs political parties
and the nominating conventions.

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:17.479
Right.

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:19.489
So it's just a matter of the parties deciding
to make changes.

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:24.770
And they could do some interparty compacts
to use to to guarantee that each of them will

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:31.470
veto the emergence of individuals who clearly
do not support the Constitution, will not

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.860
solemnly swear to uphold free and fair elections
and the peaceful transfer of power and other

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:36.860
things.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:37.860
Speaker 1: When we talk about.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:41.150
Speaker 2: The Senate today, I think the Democratic
Party feels quite safe.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:42.150
I really do.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.380
I agree with you there.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.539
But it would feel like hubris to predict over
the next ten or 15 or 20 years if we don't

00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:50.780
clean some things up, that they could become
susceptible to demagogues.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:51.889
Speaker 1: Oh, I agree.

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:54.889
I all of my assessments are as of today.

00:04:54.889 --> 00:04:59.300
And, you know, when we hear people say when
Trump would say it, the State of the Union,

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.229
the U.S. will never be a socialist country.

00:05:01.229 --> 00:05:03.820
It's sort of like never is a long time.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:04.900
You know, maybe not.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.400
I don't think next year there's not too many
socialists right now in positions of power.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.620
But I think never and we want to be careful,
would never if we go back to 2016 a little

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:13.740
bit.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:19.930
There's a lot of blame that is often placed
for how Trump how everybody failed to stop

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:20.930
Trump.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.710
And sometimes it's, you know, James Comey
doing that extra press conference to talk

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:25.830
about Hillary's emails.

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.080
That was totally unnecessary.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.360
That was the difference maker because it was
so close.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:36.370
Or the media giving free publicity to Trump
is what allowed him to keep accreting to three

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.889
or 4% every time another primary candidate
dropped out.

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:40.979
That was the reason.

00:05:40.979 --> 00:05:46.169
Or Democrats selecting a poor candidate in
Hillary Clinton is what's to blame now when

00:05:46.169 --> 00:05:47.640
it's so close?

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.729
It's very hard to say.

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:54.730
One thing is to blame because any one of those
things could make a difference.

00:05:54.730 --> 00:06:01.600
But given that, how do we assess the lack
of ability to have stopped Trump in 2016?

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:09.880
Speaker 2: I think that responsibility goes
around everywhere to our basic education system,

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:13.479
to the structure of our news media, as you're
saying.

00:06:13.479 --> 00:06:20.139
And, you know, events like James Comey's relief
release of the emails or his judgment about

00:06:20.139 --> 00:06:21.139
that are very significant.

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:24.169
But I would again posit that.

00:06:24.169 --> 00:06:30.520
The only sure fire direction to go is for
political parties to block the emergence of

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:35.330
demagogues and authoritarians entirely from
entering the general election.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:39.490
If I'm not mistaken, all of your questions
related to the general election.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:44.840
And so there are certainly there are reforms
that can help to prevent a demagogue or authoritarian

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:48.580
within the general election from succeeding.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:54.130
But they become for their base, you know,
religious figures and addictive figures.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:59.099
So the most important thing that can be done,
if there were two things I would recommend,

00:06:59.099 --> 00:07:05.990
would be, first and foremost, that the political
parties do their job and return to the party

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:11.139
having the power and the platform, having
the power and the candidate having to adjust

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:15.419
to the platform, which I think should begin
in both parties to have statements around

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:18.370
the Constitution and the oath of office, for
example.

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:25.900
The other thing where we woefully failed is,
is in the process of impeachment, conviction

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.720
and disbarment from future office.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:36.720
The fact that after January six, after 45
years of witnessing Trump's bigoted speech

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:43.330
and damage to our our nation, that 43 senators
voted not to convict him either shows they

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.460
do not understand democracy, they do not understand
demagogues.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:51.789
They do not understand that democracies can
fail or that they succumb to the cowardice

00:07:51.789 --> 00:07:53.209
pathway of hyper partizanship.

00:07:53.209 --> 00:07:57.199
So I think both play the roles of those are
the two most important places for intervention.

00:07:57.199 --> 00:08:02.039
We could make an effort to at least educate
our politicians to understand these phenomena

00:08:02.039 --> 00:08:03.039
better.

00:08:03.039 --> 00:08:08.300
Speaker 1: You know, the interesting thing
about that is what you describe is what many

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:14.990
Bernie Sanders supporters say happened in
the 2020 primary, and they don't like it.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:20.080
And what I mean by that is there is a story
that's told and there's degrees of truth to

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:21.080
all of these things.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:28.960
Of course, that despite the people wanting
Bernie, the party coordinated.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:30.919
I'm now searching into my memory.

00:08:30.919 --> 00:08:37.099
But it was I believe Pete booted judge and
a couple other people getting out at the perfect

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:42.870
time to get Biden, South Carolina, with which
he built momentum to eventually become the

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:43.870
nominee.

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:47.160
We all know the story and a lot of people
are angry because they say now the people

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:52.480
should have the power, even if Bernie, whether
Bernie was destined to win or lose in a general

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:56.290
election, it should be the people, not the
party, that gets to decide.

00:08:56.290 --> 00:09:00.320
You're saying something quite different, which
is actually the party needs to be the safeguard.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.710
Speaker 2: I'm saying something completely
different.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:09.279
I do, I believe, powerfully in representative
government and representative government,

00:09:09.279 --> 00:09:13.210
as always, focus in representative government
with layers of democracy and layers of checks

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:18.720
and balances, has always survived and functioned
better than pure direct democracies.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:25.279
So, yes, what I would actually like to see
happen in the reform I think would solve or

00:09:25.279 --> 00:09:27.550
get close to solving the problem of demagogues.

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:33.890
Getting into the White House would be to let
the parties pick the presidential nominees

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:35.970
and let the people pick the president.

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:42.050
That is, in fact, the way it was done for
approximately 190 years in our country.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.060
And we switched that in the early 1970s.

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:47.070
And I think we're living the consequences
of making that switch.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:50.060
So I would have the people be very involved
in voting.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:53.890
The people would vote for the delegates.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:58.230
And hopefully by ranked choice voting, that
may be something you've covered on your program

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.710
or indeed indeed voting.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:06.430
And then the delegates would go a stepwise
fashion to what used to be done county conventions,

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:08.300
state conventions and the national conventions.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.690
I think today they would just go straight
to the national convention.

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:16.140
And they're they do their work just like we
elect delegates to the House of Representatives

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:17.140
in this country.

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:22.240
We elect them to go up there and do work,
which is to study the situation and make determinations.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:26.220
So the first thing these elected delegates
would do is establish the party platform,

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:30.410
and then they would determine which of the
candidates who are involved actually will

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:35.730
fulfill the mission of the party platform
and not take over the party the way Trump

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:36.730
has done.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:41.530
Most most countries in the world in variations
do something like I've just described.

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:48.500
Very few countries have direct primaries,
and I do believe they will continue to be

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:53.870
arrows in our sides and possibly fatal to
our democracy, meaning we will become a sham

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:57.580
democracy, not meaning we're going to engage
in ultimate civil war.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:01.329
But it's a sad thing for me to think will
be a sham democracy where only democracy and

00:11:01.329 --> 00:11:06.140
maimed and otherwise corrupt officials are
engineering everything behind the scenes.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:08.910
Speaker 1: To look forward a little bit.

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:14.380
I'm curious about your assessment of what
is currently happening within the Republican

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:20.339
Party in the sense that we have these very
unusual circumstances in which this demagogue,

00:11:20.339 --> 00:11:24.269
Trump, made a very early announcement that
he's running in 2024.

00:11:24.269 --> 00:11:27.589
He has barely left his home since making the
announcement.

00:11:27.589 --> 00:11:32.120
It's unclear really what the case is that
he's going to make for his next nomination.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:38.230
A guy who hasn't announced anything, Ron DeSantis
is actually leading Trump in some polling.

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:44.220
The speakership fiasco from the last week
has exposed it's no longer just pro and anti-Trump.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.900
Even within the Trump side, you've seen this
division.

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:51.060
Marjorie Taylor GREENE and Lauren Boebert
on opposing sides of that division.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.100
Is something fundamentally changing here?

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:58.470
Speaker 2: I want to ask you to say more about.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.970
Change something changing in the Republican
Party for the better or worse.

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:02.673
Is that what you're alluding to?

00:12:02.673 --> 00:12:04.750
Speaker 1: Well, better or worse is more of
a value judgment.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:08.570
But is the dynamic of it's the people for
and against Trump.

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:09.570
And it's that simple.

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:14.199
Is that dynamic changing where you're actually
getting even more fracturing, where the MAGA

00:12:14.199 --> 00:12:15.730
side is now dividing?

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:24.019
Speaker 2: Well, I think what what Trump has
done is he has opened the mainstream of the

00:12:24.019 --> 00:12:28.720
Republican Party in the nation, to some extent,
to what Nelson Rockefeller called back in

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:29.720
the 1960s.

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:33.750
This is his quotations, the lunatic fringe.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:41.019
So I think it's hard to predict the 20 Republicans
who or so who were opposing Kevin McCarthy.

00:12:41.019 --> 00:12:48.540
Yeah, I just think evidence a lot of qualities
in general that are very destructive to representative

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:49.540
government.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:51.610
It is grandstanding.

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:55.380
It is not thinking of the best interests of
the nation.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.620
It's a little bit of this lunatic fringe coming.

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:01.910
And so where things go, I am not certain.

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:06.949
I do know that we continue to have within
the Republican Party, the forces of Mitt Romney

00:13:06.949 --> 00:13:10.790
have been somewhat profoundly weakened, I
should say.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:11.790
Yeah.

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.080
So I don't know where the future of it is.

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:19.630
My real hope would be that at some point that
there will be a conservative party that splits

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:25.600
off from this Republican Party and that the
American people decide that this way of living

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:32.960
through demagoguery and lies and fear mongering
and hate mongering is not something they want

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:33.960
to do.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:34.960
But we did that experiment.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:36.650
Now we want to move on from it.

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:37.650
That's my hope.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:42.240
So But I do think Ron DeSantis, clearly there
are criticisms of him.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.329
He is not a demagogue.

00:13:44.329 --> 00:13:48.399
If you just look at him speak, will determine
that the man is does not have the rhetorical

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:52.079
talents and destructive abilities of of Trump.

00:13:52.079 --> 00:13:56.620
Some people worry he could become somewhat
authoritarian, but I've looked carefully at

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:59.950
what he's done in Florida and there's some
questions there.

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:05.149
Yeah, to my evaluation, the sentence appears
for the Constitution to be a much safer bet

00:14:05.149 --> 00:14:06.500
than Donald Trump.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:09.279
And Donald Trump, I think, is something's
happening for him.

00:14:09.279 --> 00:14:12.790
I think he's becoming somewhat deflated by
what's happening.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:19.460
And so hopefully he will be he could implode
and and become more dangerous to our democracy,

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:22.670
as we have seen with his comments about the
Constitution.

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:25.300
Yeah, but it's all quite unpredictable, as
I'm sure you agree.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:31.130
Speaker 1: When we look in history, what sort
of events have catalyzed realignments in parties

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:34.850
where parties significantly shift?

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:42.880
Speaker 2: I think what what one discovers
I'm thinking of the beginning of the Republican

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:43.880
Party in the 1850s.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:50.930
When you ask that there is a sense that there
is a national crisis, that the.

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:52.839
But someone needs to address.

00:14:52.839 --> 00:14:57.761
And the Whig Party, for example, at that time
was determined not to be willing to go in

00:14:57.761 --> 00:15:03.320
the direction that the Republican Party wanted
at that time mean it was clearly not an anti-slavery

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:06.490
platform, but it was an anti expansion of
slavery platform.

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:12.110
So a critical mass had been met there where
they felt the sickness has gone on in our

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:16.930
nation long enough and we cannot get traction
in these other parties.

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:22.720
And critically, back then, there was more
flexibility in the system.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:29.089
Certainly there was not this entrenched money
in politics and the political industrial complex

00:15:29.089 --> 00:15:31.860
that makes it so hard to form third parties.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:37.290
Now that's a bit of a challenge and it's very
worrisome that third parties can be wonderful

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:38.290
things.

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:41.209
And again, you know, the Republican Party
was a third party.

00:15:41.209 --> 00:15:44.009
Yeah, that rapidly grew and did well.

00:15:44.009 --> 00:15:45.579
So I think it's crisis.

00:15:45.579 --> 00:15:49.940
And so it could be that the current crisis
of demagoguery and authoritarianism and corruption

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:56.120
that we're beginning to witness now that could
